/-
# The moduli ideal of toric dynamical systems is a toric ideal

A mass-action system is given by a digraph `G` on finitely many nodes `V`, without loops, whose
node `i` carries the monomial `c^{y_i}` for a complex `y_i ∈ ℕ^s`, and whose edge `(i, j)` carries
a rate constant `κ_{ij}`. It is a **toric dynamical system** (complex balanced) when
`Ψ(c) A_κ = 0` has a strictly positive solution, where `A_κ` is the Laplacian of the labelled
digraph and `Ψ(c) = (c^{y_1}, …, c^{y_n})`.

For a node `i`, an `i`-tree is a spanning tree of the connected component of `i` in which every
edge is directed towards the unique sink `i`; `K_i = ∑_T κ^T` is the sum over `i`-trees `T` of
the products of their edge labels (a polynomial in the rate constants, the Matrix-Tree
polynomial). The `K_i` are algebraically independent (Lemma 5), so the subring
`ℚ[c, K] = ℚ[c_1, …, c_s, K_1, …, K_n]` of `ℚ[c, κ]` is a polynomial ring; here it is the
polynomial ring in the concentration variables `c_s` and one variable `K_i` for each node `i`.
When every connected component of `G` is strongly connected, the **toric steady state ideal**
`T_G` is the ideal of `ℚ[c, K]` obtained by saturating, with respect to `c_1 ⋯ c_s`, the ideal
generated by the binomials `K_i c^{y_j} - K_j c^{y_i}` for `i`, `j` in the same component. For a
strongly connected `G` the source calls it the **toric balancing ideal**. It is not the source's
steady state ideal `⟨Ψ(c) · A_κ · Y⟩`, a different ideal of `ℚ[c, κ]`. The **moduli ideal** is
`M_G = T_G ∩ ℚ[K]`, which the source writes `T_G ∩ ℚ[κ]` (equation (9)). The **Cayley matrix**
`Cay_G(Y)` has one column per node `j`, the complex `y_j` stacked over the indicator vector of the
component of `j`. The deficiency is `δ = n - σ - l`, where `n` is the number of nodes, `σ` the
dimension of the span of the edge vectors `y_j - y_i` and `l` the number of connected components.

**Proposition 6** (Craciun–Dickenstein–Shiu–Sturmfels 2009). For a strongly connected `G`, the
toric balancing ideal `T_G` is a toric ideal in `ℚ[c, K]`, generated by the binomials
`K^{u+} c^{(uY)-} - K^{u-} c^{(uY)+}` for the row vectors `u ∈ ℤ^n` whose coordinates sum to
zero.

**Theorem 9** (Craciun–Dickenstein–Shiu–Sturmfels 2009). The moduli ideal `M_G` equals the toric
ideal of the Cayley matrix `Cay_G(Y)`: it is the ideal of `ℚ[K]` generated by all binomials
`K^u - K^v` with `u, v ∈ ℕ^n` and `Cay_G(Y) (u - v) = 0`. The codimension of this toric ideal
equals the deficiency `δ`.

The source defines `T_G = ⟨1⟩` (so `M_G = ⟨1⟩`) when some component is not strongly connected;
the theorem is stated here for digraphs whose components are strongly connected. Proposition 6
describes the source's definition and is not stated here. Rate constants are indexed by all
ordered pairs of nodes; only the edges occur in the `K_i`. The toric ideal is
`MathlibExt.RingTheory.ToricIdeal.toricIdeal`.

Sources: G. Craciun, A. Dickenstein, A. Shiu, B. Sturmfels, "Toric dynamical systems", J.
Symbolic Comput. 44 (2009) 1551–1565, Section 2 (Lemma 5, Proposition 6, equation (9)) and
Theorem 9 (arXiv:0708.3431).
-/
module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import MathlibExt.RingTheory.ToricIdeal
public import Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Rename
public import Mathlib.Combinatorics.SimpleGraph.Connectivity.Connected
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dimension.Finrank
public import Mathlib.Logic.Function.Iterate
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Colon
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Height

@[expose] public section

namespace WantedExt.Biology.Biophysics.ToricDynamicalSystemsWanted

open MvPolynomial

section MassActionGraphs

/-- A mass-action graph: a digraph on the nodes `V` with edge set `E` and no loops, whose node `j`
carries the complex `Y j ∈ ℕ^Sp`. -/
structure MassActionGraph (V Sp : Type*) where
  /-- The edges `(i, j)`, written `i → j`. -/
  E : Set (V × V)
  /-- There are no loops. -/
  irrefl : ∀ i, (i, i) ∉ E
  /-- The complex carried by each node. -/
  Y : V → Sp → ℕ

namespace MassActionGraph

variable {V Sp : Type*} (G : MassActionGraph V Sp)

/-- The underlying undirected graph. -/
def undirected : SimpleGraph V :=
  SimpleGraph.fromRel fun i j => (i, j) ∈ G.E

/-- `i` and `j` lie in the same connected component. -/
def Linked (i j : V) : Prop :=
  G.undirected.Reachable i j

/-- Every connected component is strongly connected: nodes in the same component are joined by a
directed path. -/
def ComponentsStronglyConnected : Prop :=
  ∀ i j, G.Linked i j → Relation.ReflTransGen (fun a b => (a, b) ∈ G.E) i j

/-- `t` encodes an `i`-tree: a spanning tree of the component of `i` in which `j ≠ i` has the
unique outgoing edge `j → t j` and every node reaches the sink `i` by iterating `t`; nodes
outside the component are fixed. -/
def IsInTree (i : V) (t : V → V) : Prop :=
  t i = i ∧ (∀ j, ¬ G.Linked j i → t j = j) ∧
    ∀ j, G.Linked j i → (j ≠ i → (j, t j) ∈ G.E) ∧ ∃ m, t^[m] j = i

open scoped Classical in
/-- For a finite vertex set `V` (`[Fintype V]`), the Matrix-Tree polynomial
`K_i = ∑_{T an i-tree} κ^T` in the rate constants `κ_{jk}`, where `κ^T` is the product of the
`κ_{jk}` over the edges `j → k` of `T`. The `K_i` are algebraically independent (Lemma 5 of the
source), so the source's subring `ℚ[c, K]` of `ℚ[c, κ]` is a polynomial ring: the toric steady
state ideal and the moduli ideal are defined in the polynomial ring with one variable `K_i` per
node. -/
noncomputable def treePoly [Fintype V] (i : V) : MvPolynomial (V × V) ℚ :=
  ∑ t ∈ Finset.univ.filter (G.IsInTree i),
    ∏ j ∈ Finset.univ.filter (fun j => G.Linked j i ∧ j ≠ i), X (j, t j)

end MassActionGraph

end MassActionGraphs

section ToricSteadyStateIdeal

namespace MassActionGraph

variable {V Sp : Type*} (G : MassActionGraph V Sp)

/-- For a finite species set `Sp` (`[Fintype Sp]`), the monomial
`c^{y_j} = ∏_{s : Sp} c_s ^ (y_j s)` in the concentration variables of `ℚ[c, K]`: the polynomial
ring in the variables `c_s = X (Sum.inl s)` and one variable `K_i = X (Sum.inr i)` for each node
`i`. -/
noncomputable def complexMonomial [Fintype Sp] (j : V) : MvPolynomial (Sp ⊕ V) ℚ :=
  ∏ s, X (Sum.inl s) ^ G.Y j s

/-- For a finite species set `Sp` (`[Fintype Sp]`), the ideal of `ℚ[c, K]` generated by the
binomials `K_i c^{y_j} - K_j c^{y_i}` for `i`, `j` in the same connected component, where
`K_i = X (Sum.inr i)` is the variable of the node `i`. -/
noncomputable def binomialIdeal [Fintype Sp] : Ideal (MvPolynomial (Sp ⊕ V) ℚ) :=
  Ideal.span {p | ∃ i j, G.Linked i j ∧
    p = X (Sum.inr i) * G.complexMonomial j - X (Sum.inr j) * G.complexMonomial i}

/-- For a finite species set `Sp` (`[Fintype Sp]`), the toric steady state ideal `T_G` of
`ℚ[c, K]`: the saturation of the ideal generated by the binomials `K_i c^{y_j} - K_j c^{y_i}`, for
`i`, `j` in the same connected component, with respect to the product `c_1 ⋯ c_s` of the
concentration variables. When every connected component is strongly connected this is the
source's toric steady state ideal `(T_{G_1} + ⋯ + T_{G_l}) : (c_1 ⋯ c_s)^∞`, and for a strongly
connected `G` its toric balancing ideal. The source sets `T_G = ⟨1⟩` when some component is not
strongly connected; this definition does not. It is not the source's steady state ideal
`⟨Ψ(c) · A_κ · Y⟩`, which is a different ideal of `ℚ[c, κ]`. -/
noncomputable def toricSteadyStateIdeal [Fintype Sp] : Ideal (MvPolynomial (Sp ⊕ V) ℚ) :=
  ⨆ N : ℕ, G.binomialIdeal.colon {(∏ s, X (Sum.inl s)) ^ N}

/-- For a finite species set `Sp` (`[Fintype Sp]`), the moduli ideal `M_G = T_G ∩ ℚ[K]`: the
polynomials in the variables `K_i` alone that lie in the toric steady state ideal `T_G`. The
source writes `M_G = T_G ∩ ℚ[κ]`, reading `K_i` as the tree polynomial `K_i(κ)`; as
`ℚ[c, K] ∩ ℚ[κ] = ℚ[K]`, this is the same ideal. -/
noncomputable def moduliIdeal [Fintype Sp] : Ideal (MvPolynomial V ℚ) :=
  G.toricSteadyStateIdeal.comap (rename Sum.inr)

end MassActionGraph

end ToricSteadyStateIdeal

section CayleyMatrix

namespace MassActionGraph

variable {V Sp : Type*} (G : MassActionGraph V Sp)

/-- For a finite species set `Sp` (`[Fintype Sp]`), the columns of the Cayley matrix `Cay_G(Y)`:
the complex `y_j` stacked over the indicator vector of the connected component of `j`. -/
noncomputable def cayley [Fintype Sp] (j : V) : Sp ⊕ G.undirected.ConnectedComponent →₀ ℕ :=
  (Finsupp.equivFunOnFinite.symm (G.Y j)).mapDomain Sum.inl +
    Finsupp.single (Sum.inr (G.undirected.connectedComponentMk j)) 1

/-- For a finite vertex set `V` (`[Fintype V]`), the deficiency `δ = n - σ - l`: the number of
nodes `|V|`, minus the `ℚ`-dimension of the span of the edge vectors `y_j - y_i`, minus the number
of connected components. -/
noncomputable def deficiency [Fintype V] : ℤ :=
  (Fintype.card V : ℤ) -
    Module.finrank ℚ (Submodule.span ℚ
      ((fun e : V × V => fun s => (G.Y e.2 s : ℚ) - G.Y e.1 s) '' G.E)) -
    Nat.card G.undirected.ConnectedComponent

end MassActionGraph

end CayleyMatrix

section Theorem

open MassActionGraph MathlibExt.RingTheory.ToricIdeal

/-- **The moduli ideal is toric** (Craciun–Dickenstein–Shiu–Sturmfels 2009, Theorem 9, first
part). For a mass-action graph whose connected components are strongly connected, with finitely
many nodes (`[Fintype V]`) and species (`[Fintype Sp]`), the moduli ideal `M_G = T_G ∩ ℚ[K]`
equals the toric ideal of the Cayley matrix `Cay_G(Y)`: the ideal of `ℚ[K]` generated by the
binomials `K^u - K^v` with `u, v ∈ ℕ^n` and `Cay_G(Y) u = Cay_G(Y) v`.

Source: G. Craciun, A. Dickenstein, A. Shiu, B. Sturmfels, J. Symbolic Comput. 44 (2009)
1551–1565, Theorem 9. -/
theorem_wanted moduliIdeal_eq_toricIdeal {V Sp : Type*} [Fintype V] [Fintype Sp]
    (G : MassActionGraph V Sp) (hG : G.ComponentsStronglyConnected) :
    G.moduliIdeal = toricIdeal ℚ G.cayley

/-- **Codimension of the moduli ideal** (Craciun–Dickenstein–Shiu–Sturmfels 2009, Theorem 9,
second part). For a mass-action graph whose connected components are strongly connected, with
finitely many nodes (`[Fintype V]`) and species (`[Fintype Sp]`), the codimension (height) of the
toric ideal of the Cayley matrix in `ℚ[K_1, …, K_n]` equals the deficiency `δ`.

Source: G. Craciun, A. Dickenstein, A. Shiu, B. Sturmfels, J. Symbolic Comput. 44 (2009)
1551–1565, Theorem 9. -/
theorem_wanted height_toricIdeal_cayley {V Sp : Type*} [Fintype V] [Fintype Sp]
    (G : MassActionGraph V Sp) (hG : G.ComponentsStronglyConnected) :
    ((toricIdeal ℚ G.cayley).height : ℕ∞) = (G.deficiency.toNat : ℕ∞) ∧ 0 ≤ G.deficiency

end Theorem

end WantedExt.Biology.Biophysics.ToricDynamicalSystemsWanted
